      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T16:53:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Can you elaborate on why we\u0027d want to do this?\n\nSigned container sizes don\u0027t make much sense, and we\u0027re also increasing the impedance mismatch with all non-skia containers?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2540cd0f_89f6d5e7",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T16:56:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Herb is working on reducing the number of containers we have in Skia, and a starting point is to come up with a common API surface. (Presumably once two containers have matching APIs, one can be substituted for another easily.) Here\u0027s Herb\u0027s doc\n\nhttp://doc/1GiyzXWqviHV11TkrSp1qkbJ_HrJlFVyCDiGPrdcexRE#heading\u003dh.k77d45qsa47t\n\nOTOH, I have to say I do agree with the impedance mismatch concerns, and this CL is a good example of what those concerns look like. Just because we store an int internally does not mean we need to return an int. That\u0027s why we have historically had both `size` (impedance match) and `count` (Skia-specific).",
      "parentUuid": "54aa079a_ff399966",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b47f68e_7c80dce7",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5642
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T17:00:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks, that should be added to the description.\n\nWould it make sense to go the other way and convert all skia containers to size_t?  Seems odd to move in the wrong direction even for the right reason :)",
      "parentUuid": "2540cd0f_89f6d5e7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ef484290_d668fbb1",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T17:05:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we\u0027ve prioritized the goal of \"containers are as small as we can make them\" over the goal of \"containers can store \u003e2 billion elements.\" For the rare cases where we might have billions of items in a container, we can use `vector\u003c\u003e`.\n\nFor signed-vs-unsigned, there is a case to be made that STL choosing unsigned for container sizes was a mistake. It\u0027s brittle/makes it hard to detect overflow. However, I don\u0027t know how compelling that argument actually is, vs the reality of \"we still need to interact with STL.\"",
      "parentUuid": "4b47f68e_7c80dce7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1401239c_af47b642",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T17:33:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "From Google\u0027s c++ style guide:\nUnsigned integers are good for representing bitfields and modular arithmetic. Because of historical accident, the C++ standard also uses unsigned integers to represent the size of containers - many members of the standards body believe this to be a mistake, but it is effectively impossible to fix at this point.\n...\nDo not use an unsigned type merely to assert that a variable is non-negative.\n\nUnsigned values are more dangerous than int, and in Skia they introduce the impedance mismatch.\n\nThere are three several types used for representing the count of things in the skia codebase: size_t, unsigned, uint32_t, and int. The fact that int can be checked by UBSAN for overflow is a pretty compelling safeguard.\n\nThe skia collections all seem to have sizes bounded by [0, MAX_INT]. Therefore, the transitive closure set of values that feed into the administrative methods of the skia collections should be int. Having unsigned, etc. count doesn\u0027t buy you anything because they are all limited to the range [0, MAX_INT].\n\nWhy not have size_t containers? Because there are zero use cases in Skia that seem to demand it. This causes waste. A vector is 50% bigger than SkTArray.\n\nBecause of unsigned ints guarantee of modular arithmetic, sometime more code is needed to to keep that promise.\n\nIf you do use mixed std collections and skia collections, int is always safe for both.",
      "parentUuid": "ef484290_d668fbb1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3fd434a5_9490a88c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T18:03:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I missed an additional reason why I think int is a better choice than size_t. Size_t means different things on different platforms.\n  * x86_64 size_t - unsigned 64-bit number.\n  * arm size_t - unsigned 32-bit number and has the same type as uint32_t.\n  * wasm32 size_t - unsigned 32-bit number and has a *different* type than \n    uint32_t.\n    \nThis difference creates many problems with interface and thinking about overflow.\n\nint is the same everywhere. I can think about it one way, and it translates to all the platforms.",
      "parentUuid": "1401239c_af47b642",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2ba7234a_4cf03ed0",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5642
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T18:49:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e From Google\u0027s c++ style guide:\n\u003e Unsigned integers are good for representing bitfields and modular arithmetic. Because of historical accident, the C++ standard also uses unsigned integers to represent the size of containers - many members of the standards body believe this to be a mistake, but it is effectively impossible to fix at this point.\n\u003e ...\n\u003e Do not use an unsigned type merely to assert that a variable is non-negative.\n\nYet every collection impl under the sun, including Google\u0027s, seems to use unsigned types for size :)\n\nI find that last sentence particularly odd as it flat out dismisses one of the basic principles (pick a type that reflects your data domain as accurately as possible).  We\u0027re not merely asserting that the variable is non-negative, we\u0027re ensuring it\u0027s impossible to represent a negative value... which doesn\u0027t hurt when range-checking or defending against OOB access. /shrug/\n\n\n\u003e The fact that int can be checked by UBSAN for overflow is a pretty compelling safeguard.\n\nUBSAN can also check unsigned overflow, no?  -fsanitize\u003dunsigned-integer-overflow\n\n\n\u003e A vector is 50% bigger than SkTArray.\n\nOh, ok, so SkTArray already stores an int size internally (and takes int indices).  For some reason I though we\u0027re also changing that part.\n\nUpdating size() to match the current representation seems fine.",
      "parentUuid": "1401239c_af47b642",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4f87bf61_7c4321b5",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T18:54:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are their any additional objections? Is this resolved?",
      "parentUuid": "3fd434a5_9490a88c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "267bd327_bd4db427",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T19:12:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The fact that int can be checked by UBSAN for overflow is a pretty compelling safeguard.\n\nUBSAN can also check unsigned overflow, no? -fsanitize\u003dunsigned-integer-overflow\n\nThe problem is that we use unsigned integer overflow in our code like for checking for overflow. So, we would have to selectively address each of those instances.",
      "parentUuid": "4f87bf61_7c4321b5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9fe1ef80_1163b436",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T19:22:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think there are two changes which are being conflated.\n\n- What type should we use to store the count? AFAICS everyone agrees `int` works great. It\u0027s smaller and UBSAN works.\n\n- What API should expose the count to outside code? Personally I am not yet convinced that this CL is better than what we have today, which is: `count()` returns that int as-is, and `size()` returns a size_t for easy mix-and-match with existing libraries.\n\nAll of the discussion seems to center on the first point but let\u0027s talk about the second point too. I am not sure why we can\u0027t have our cake and eat it too and just expose both `size` and `count`.",
      "parentUuid": "267bd327_bd4db427",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ae96be12_91cdc9e0",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T20:19:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "* SkTDArray has always been 16 bytes.\n* People really don\u0027t mix and match. For whatever reason, they have decided to have external counts or indexes as unsigned or uint32_t.\n\nFor example all the index in the paragraph code are size_t or unsigned,  but the data is backed by SkTArray. Just changing the those to int makes the code smaller, read easier, and safer. \n\nAnother example is     \nstruct ConditionalOpCounts {\n        size_t numReachableOps;\n        size_t numStoreOps;\n};\n\nThe things they count are fReachableOps and fStoreOps which are both SkTArrays. There is no reason they should have been size_t instead of int.\n\nAnother example is SkSVGDevice.cpp, there is no need for rec_idx to be size_t. It should have been int from the start.\n\nThe above list of files have very few cross std collection to skia collection linkages.\n\nAnything that ends up being an index into SkTArray should be an int.\n\nHaving both count and size produces larger binaries, and creates confusion about which call to use. Having size() return int is a strong signal that this is an int sized collection.\n\nAnd, it\u0027s nice to have c.resize(c.size() + 1) not need cast.",
      "parentUuid": "9fe1ef80_1163b436",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2277dc6b_bb9f8f81",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T20:56:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m also going to throw out this: I don\u0027t think interop with std:: containers is *that* important or likely, particularly if we succeed in our other goals. Namely: We don\u0027t want clients using our containers. Given that, if we can use int internally everywhere, get the size/code-size/consistency... benefits, and live with the limitations, it seems fine.\n\nI think the one exception where we want to be careful is SkSpan -- in my ideal world, our clients wouldn\u0027t know about Sk[TDS]*Array, and as much as possibly we\u0027d be using SkSpan to accept value ranges at the API boundary. That means that SkSpan becomes the one bridge that needs to speak both int and size_t (assuming that clients have std:: structures they\u0027re using to construct the spans they pass in) ... but having a single funnel lets us decide which type to store in spans, and where/how to do the conversion.",
      "parentUuid": "ae96be12_91cdc9e0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "537bf7f0_cf29db91",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T21:05:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`SkSpan` is probably temporary, like `StringFragment` was. It only exists at all because C++ doesn\u0027t support `std::span` until C++20. I expect we will migrate to `std::span` when we migrate from C++17 to C++20. If we change the `size()` API, now we will run into trouble when we try to transition to `std::span`.",
      "parentUuid": "2277dc6b_bb9f8f81",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5506f260_b0fdfc2d",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T21:14:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not a fan of borrowing trouble from the future. But, wouldn\u0027t mean we just introduce the ctor SkTArray(std::span\u003cT\u003e s)?",
      "parentUuid": "537bf7f0_cf29db91",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5e0b9f1f_fbc66a77",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
