    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d59e2a2_b63cbffa",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T21:20:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, in Brian\u0027s design, clients wouldn\u0027t be using SkTArray, so AFAICS that would not be an issue. The concern I\u0027m thinking of would be that there is already existing client code which uses SkSpan and is warning-safe with its size() returning size_t. If we update its size() to return int, we could introduce warnings in client code and cause churn to fix it. Then if we switch to std::span\u003cT\u003e in the future, we will whiplash back to the original state anyway, meaning all churn was just wasted effort.",
      "parentUuid": "5506f260_b0fdfc2d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6a3dcd44_c8c47f31",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 7,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T21:22:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, I think Herb\u0027s vision makes sense - we keep SkSpan compatible with std::span, and do the translation/checking when going between that and any internal containers. That gives us the insulation we need to do whatever we want internally.",
      "parentUuid": "5d59e2a2_b63cbffa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 7,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "50994678_06a58374",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T17:10:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I just had a thought--what about `std::size(c)`? We can\u0027t control what it returns, it\u0027s always going to be `size_t` (built-in function). Does this mean we can no longer call it on an `SkTArray` without causing a warning? The ability to write generic code that targets Skia containers and built-in containers has a _ton of value_.\n\nActually, is this why `SkSpan` broke?",
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "34603b18_02ed3970",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5210
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-07T17:33:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "std::size(c) does *not* always return size_t. That is why SkSpan is broken.\n\nHere is the C++17 definition of size().\n\ntemplate\u003c class C \u003e\nconstexpr auto size( const C\u0026 c ) -\u003e decltype(c.size());\n\nIt returns whatever the underlying size of the container returns.",
      "parentUuid": "50994678_06a58374",
      "revId": "12b01ae5d7552829da5c03fb2a54e74869f75815",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
